## Introduction

In this lab, I implemented a **trace** system call to log system calls for debugging and exploited a security vulnerability in xv6 to reveal a secret from another process. This helped me understand how system calls work and how bugs can break isolation boundaries.

---

## Lab Tasks

### Using GDB
I used **GDB** to debug the xv6 kernel and answered questions about the kernel's behavior during system calls. This helped me understand how traps and system calls are handled in xv6.

---

### System Call Tracing
I implemented a **trace** system call that logs system calls based on a mask. This allowed me to trace specific system calls and understand how they interact with the kernel.

---

### Attack xv6
I exploited a security vulnerability in xv6 to reveal a secret from another process. This task taught me how system call bugs can be exploited to break isolation boundaries.

---

## Building and Running xv6

1. **Prerequisites**:  
   - I installed the RISC-V toolchain from [riscv-gnu-toolchain](https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain).
   - I installed QEMU for RISC-V (`riscv64-softmmu`).

2. **Build xv6**:
   $ make qemu

Testing and Grading
I used cowtest to test my COW implementation.
I ran usertests -q to ensure my implementation didn't break other functionality.
